## Phase 22d — the responsive pass, and a walkthrough somebody else can drive / 2026-09-07 / (this commit)

### Built
`public/css/tokens.css` — a BREAKPOINTS block: `--bp-sm: 640px`, `--bp-md:
1024px`, `--bp-lg: 1440px`, and the note that a media query cannot read a custom
property, so the three numbers are written out literally and this block is what
says what they mean.

`public/css/field.css` — the field column now steps 100% → 40rem (640) → 60rem
(1024) → 66rem (1440); page padding moved off Bootstrap's `p-3` onto a
`--page-pad-x` that takes `env(safe-area-inset-left/right)`; a single rule
putting every `.btn`, `.form-control` and `.form-select` on the two field
surfaces at `min-height: 48px`.

`public/css/driver.css` — `[data-panel="driver-status"]` becomes a two-column
grid at 1024. `public/driver.html` gained two wrappers, `.driver-aside` (map +
progress) and `.driver-rest` (everything under the hero).

`public/css/technician.css` — `[data-panel="ladder"]` becomes a two-column grid
at 1024 when there is a map to put in the second column;
`public/js/panels/technicianLadder.js` moves the map out of the job card to a
sibling (`.job-map`) and marks whatever the next action turns out to be
(`.job-action`).

`public/css/console-regions.css` — three columns at 1440, two at 1024 with
`.console-investigate` spanning both, one below; a below-640 block that stacks
the timeline filters and contains the wide tables' scroll inside their own card.

`public/css/console.css` — `.metric-grid` (5 → 3 → 2 → 1 tiles) and
`.stack-table`, which turns a table into a list of cards below 640;
`public/js/panels/fleetDashboard.js` writes the `data-label` and `is-heading`
attributes it reads.

`public/css/components.css` — `.actionbar` clears `env(safe-area-inset-bottom)`;
the app bar's duty toggle and "All surfaces" link get a 48px hit band from a
pseudo-element without changing their ink.

`scripts/demo-open-shifts.ts` — widens every technician's current shift to cover
the next twelve hours.

`WALKTHROUGH.md` — the self-driven run, with what to expect at every step.

### Decisions

**Decision** Three breakpoints, declared once in `tokens.css`, used literally.
**Why** There were four numbers on the pages before this — 430, 1200, 1600 and
Bootstrap's own 576/768/992/1200/1400 through `col-md-*` — and none of them
agreed with any other. A layout that changes at five widths is a layout nobody
can hold in their head.
**Alternative** Adopt Bootstrap's breakpoints, since the framework is already
loaded.
**Why not** They are wrong for this product: 992 is their laptop step and the
dispatcher's three columns genuinely do not fit until 1440, so following them
would mean crushing the console to honour a number chosen for somebody else's
grid.
**Tradeoff** The `--bp-*` custom properties look usable in a media query and are
not. That trap is the reason the comment above them is six lines long.

**Decision** The field surfaces widen only where a second column earns it, and
each two-column region is placed EXPLICITLY — every child assigned a row and a
column.
**Why** Auto-placement across two columns is what produced the first version's
260px hole under the driver's hero: an item with a definite column and an
automatic row shares the placement cursor with the left-hand stack, and the
cursor never moves backwards. Placing the aside definitely takes it out of that
pass entirely.
**Alternative** `grid-auto-flow: row dense`.
**Why not** It works, and it is a rule nobody reading this file could predict
the effect of. Two explicit placements beat one clever keyword — the readability
contract, applied to CSS.
**Tradeoff** Two new wrapper divs in `driver.html` that exist for layout alone.
Each carries a comment saying so.

**Decision** The technician's sticky action bar stops being sticky at 1024.
**Why** A thumb bar pinned to the bottom of a laptop window sits a long way from
the card it belongs to, and its fade-out gradient becomes a smear across the
middle of the page. In the right-hand column it is simply the button, under the
map.
**Alternative** Keep it sticky everywhere.
**Why not** Sticky solves "a phone is held at the bottom". Nothing is held at
the bottom of a laptop.
**Tradeoff** Two behaviours for one component, which is the thing this pass
otherwise avoids. It is one `position: static` inside one media query.

**Decision** The two app-bar controls get a 48px TARGET from a pseudo-element
rather than 48px of height.
**Why** Every control on a field surface clears 48px; these two cannot do it
with their own box, because the app bar is sticky and a 48px pill makes a 72px
header that costs a tenth of a phone screen on every scroll of every page. WCAG
2.5.5 measures the target, not the ink.
**Alternative** Make them 48px tall and accept the header.
**Why not** 72px against 844px is 8.5% of the viewport, permanently.
**Tradeoff** An audit that measures `getBoundingClientRect().height` reports both
as failures. Ours did; the answer is a second probe with `elementFromPoint` at
±23px, which both pass.

**Decision** The fleet tables become stacked cards below 640 in CSS, with the
labels supplied by `data-label` attributes rather than by a second rendering
path.
**Why** One markup path means a screen reader still gets a table, and there is
one place that builds a row.
**Alternative** Render cards on a phone and a table above it, from the panel.
**Why not** Two renderers of the same data is the shape this codebase keeps
finding bugs in.
**Tradeoff** Every `<td>` now carries a `data-label` that is invisible above
640, and a column added without one renders an unlabelled value on a phone.

### Concurrency
Nothing. This phase adds no route, no write, no queue and no server code. The one
new script (`demo-open-shifts.ts`) writes `technician_shifts` directly and is
demo prep, not production: it is not idempotent in the useful sense — running it
twice extends the window twice — and it is documented as such.

### Weaknesses introduced
- `body.driver` and `body.technician` widen at 1024 whether or not the wide
  content is on screen. On the driver's REQUEST flow, before a job exists, the
  column is 60rem of one-question-at-a-time screens; the questions themselves
  are constrained by their own components, but the page is wider than the
  reading it holds. Does not matter much — the request flow lasts under a
  minute — but it is the one place the extra width is not earned.
- `.console .card-body { overflow-x: auto }` below 640 applies to every card
  body on the dispatcher, not only the two that need it, and `overflow-x: auto`
  forces `overflow-y` to compute to `auto` as well — so every card is
  technically a scroll container on a phone. Harmless today because nothing
  there is taller than its content; it would stop being harmless if a card ever
  wanted a sticky child.
- The `:has()` guards on both field grids mean the layout depends on an element
  being present and unhidden. That is correct, and it is also a second thing
  deciding layout alongside the media query. A panel that starts rendering its
  map into a different container would silently lose its second column.
- The audit that produced these results is a throwaway script in a scratch
  directory, not a test. Nothing in the repo will notice if a future change
  reintroduces a horizontal scrollbar at 390px.

### Concepts now genuinely in use
CSS grid explicit placement, and specifically why definite-position items are
placed before the auto-placement pass; `env()` safe-area insets and the
`viewport-fit=cover` that makes them non-zero; touch-target size as distinct
from rendered size; `:has()` as a layout guard.

### What broke
**The driver's live panel opened with a 260px hole under the hero.** The aside
was pinned to `grid-row: 1`, so row one was as tall as the map and the progress
list together — 454px against a 191px hero — and everything in the left column
started below it. Fixed by giving the panel three children instead of many and
letting the aside span both rows.

**A driver who RESUMES a job receives no live updates at all.** Found while
verifying the walkthrough's central claim, not by looking for it.
`public/js/pages/driver.js` calls `subscribeToJob` only on the create path (line
370); neither `restoreOpenJob` nor the resume picker's handler does, so a driver
who signs in again — or simply reloads — holds a socket that connects, reports
"Connected", and joins no job room. Measured with CDP frame logging: 0
`job:event` frames across a whole run after resuming, 15 across the same run when
the job was created in that window. NOT FIXED — outside this phase's scope, and
written up in `WALKTHROUGH.md` and here so it is not lost.

**Selecting a plan does not offer the job to anybody.** Also found while writing
the walkthrough. `selectPlan` writes decision records and a `PLAN_SELECTED`
event; the only path to a `dispatch_offer` is `executeReassignment`, which needs
a `REASSIGN_*` action, which needs a replan trigger. So "solve, then accept the
offer" is not a sequence the product supports — the walkthrough goes through a
driver's safety message instead. Correct as built, and surprising enough to be
worth writing down.

**The board is time-of-day dependent.** At 23:50 IST four of thirty-one
technicians were on shift and 22 of 24 jobs came back unassignable. The seed's
shift bands are realistic and right for the evaluation harness, which fixes its
own clock; they are wrong for somebody sitting down to drive the product at an
arbitrary hour. `scripts/demo-open-shifts.ts` is the workaround, not a fix.

**`clientSequence: Date.now()` overflows an INT4.** A harness bug rather than a
product one, but it surfaced as a bare `internal_error` from
`POST /jobs/:id/actions`, with the real cause — a Prisma `ConversionError` —
visible only in the server log. The route could say more.

### Interview angle
"How do you decide what a wider screen is FOR?" — answerable with the driver's
panel: the extra width went to the map and the progress list, which are
reassurance, so that the headline, the arrival time and the person are all on
screen at once without scrolling; and with the technician's, where the person
card and the one thing to press ended up side by side. And: "what does a
responsive pass have to do with accessibility?" — the 48px floor, and the
difference between a control's ink and its target, which is the whole reason the
duty toggle is 36px and passes anyway.

