## Phase 22f — making a correct decision visible / 2026-09-07 / (this commit)

### Built
`src/commit/redispatch.ts` — `escalateNoFeasiblePlan()`. The no-option branch
was `return { outcome: 'NO_OPTION', solverRunId }` and nothing else; it now
writes a `NO_FEASIBLE_PLAN` job event and raises an `ESCALATE_NO_FEASIBLE_PLAN`
action carrying the rejection breakdown, guarded by `hasOpenAction` so a job
whose triggers keep firing raises one card rather than twenty.

`src/domain/escalationCard.ts` — `describeRejectionCounts()`, the one-line
breakdown (`NO_TECHNICIAN_ON_SHIFT x23, SLA_UNREACHABLE x1`) that goes on the
worklist row.

`src/domain/actionTiers.ts` — `ESCALATE_NO_FEASIBLE_PLAN`, RECOMMEND.
`public/js/panels/actions.js` — its label.

`src/domain/geo.ts` — `SERVICE_AREA_RADIUS_KM = 80`.
`src/intake/createJob.ts` — `resolveZoneId` enforces it.
`src/intake/errors.ts` — `OUTSIDE_SERVICE_AREA` (422) and `driverMessageFor()`.
`src/api/intake.ts`, `public/js/api.js` — a driver-readable `message` alongside
the code, and the client preferring it.

`src/domain/healthLadder.ts` — a `CREW_ON_SHIFT` component and a
`dispatchContinues` field on every ladder entry.
`src/health/probes.ts` — `probeCrewOnShift`.
`public/js/panels/health.js` — the dev-build hint naming the shift script.

`public/js/panels/plans.js` — the button reads "Record this decision", and a
line under the cards says where dispatch actually happens.
`src/workers/solver.ts` — the header comment corrected.

`INTERVIEW.md` §15.41–43 and §16. `tests/redispatch.test.ts` and
`tests/intake.test.ts` gain a regression test each.

### Decisions

**Decision** The no-crew escalation is RECOMMEND, not APPROVE.
**Why** APPROVE renders a sign-off button wired to `POST /actions/:id/approve`,
and approving this would resolve nothing — the decision it is about (external
provider, approve anyway, call the driver) is taken on the escalation card
through its own route. Execute here means "seen, I am dealing with it", which is
what the SLA card's button already means.
**Alternative** APPROVE, on the grounds that a job with no crew is exactly the
sort of thing that should block.
**Why not** It is already blocked — by there being nobody to send. Adding a
sign-off gate would make the tier decorative.
**Tradeoff** Two RECOMMEND kinds whose Execute does not execute anything. The
tier is now carrying two meanings: "do this" and "acknowledge this".

**Decision** The breakdown is reason CODES on an operator surface, not prose.
**Why** `NO_TECHNICIAN_ON_SHIFT x23` is scannable and diagnostic; twenty-three
copies of "the units that could do this work have no technician on shift" is
not. The plain-language sentence is one line further down the same card.
**Alternative** Words everywhere, per the console's own "no enum on screen" rule.
**Why not** That rule exists for the DRIVER's surface. `panels/actions.js`
already argues the opposite for the escalation card's feasibility code, and this
is the same argument one level up.
**Tradeoff** A code on screen is a code somebody has to have seen before.

**Decision** 80km, enforced at intake rather than in routing.
**Why** OSRM snapping an off-graph coordinate onto its extract is correct
behaviour for a router handed a coordinate it should never have received. The
input is where it can be rejected honestly.
**Alternative** Have `routing.ts` refuse coordinates outside its extract's
bounding box.
**Why not** It would need to know the extract's geography, which it deliberately
does not, and it would fire long after a job had been created and queued.
**Tradeoff** 80km is generous enough that a genuinely unreachable job 70km out
is still accepted and will simply have a long travel time. That is the correct
direction to be wrong in.

**Decision** `CREW_ON_SHIFT` is DEGRADED, never CRITICAL.
**Why** The ladder's own definition: CRITICAL means the system has stopped
deciding. Here it is deciding correctly and there is nobody to decide about.
DISPATCH_CORE stays the only component that can go critical.
**Alternative** CRITICAL, since nothing can be dispatched.
**Why not** It would make "critical" mean two different things and would fire
every night on a real roster.
**Tradeoff** A fleet with nobody on shift reports at the same severity as a
degraded symptom assistant. The `dispatchContinues` field is what stops the
banner reading identically for both.

### Concurrency
Nothing new. The one guard that matters is `hasOpenAction` before raising the
escalation, and it is a read-then-write with a window: two triggers landing in
the same instant can both pass it and raise two cards. Deliberately not closed
with a unique index — the existing thrash escalation has the identical shape and
the failure mode is a duplicate row in a worklist, not a duplicate dispatch.

### Weaknesses introduced
- The escalation fires only on the TRIGGER path. `POST /solve` can leave twenty
  jobs unplanned and raises nothing, because a card per unplanned job on every
  whole-board solve would flood the queue it is meant to make readable. So the
  board-level version of this finding is still invisible; the `CREW_ON_SHIFT`
  banner covers the common cause and nothing covers the rest.
- `FLEET_IS_THIN_BELOW = 0.5` is a chosen figure and is labelled as one. Zero
  on shift is the non-arbitrary reading; "half" exists so a fleet at 1 of 24
  reports as starved rather than merely thin.
- The probe runs two `unit.count` queries every fifteen seconds, one with a
  nested relation filter on shifts. Cheap at 24 units and not free at 24,000.
- `driverMessageFor` is a one-element set. It will grow, and when it does the
  question of which failures deserve a sentence needs answering deliberately
  rather than by adding to a list.
- The plans panel now explains a gap rather than closing it. Honest, and still
  a paragraph of prose where a wired button belongs.

### What broke
**The new health component made the banner's standing claim false.** The
degraded headline ended "Dispatch decisions are unaffected", which was true of
all five components that existed when it was written and is exactly wrong for a
fleet with nobody on shift. Caught by reading the live banner after wiring the
probe, not by a test. Fixed with a `dispatchContinues` field per ladder entry so
the tail is read off the components rather than asserted — and so the next
component added has to answer the question.

**A regression test asserted the fixture rather than the behaviour.** The first
version expected `reasonCode` to be `NO_TECHNICIAN_ON_SHIFT`; the redispatch
fixture's job needs a flatbed, so most of the seeded fleet is also
`VEHICLE_TYPE_INCOMPATIBLE` and that code dominated. Rewritten to assert the
counts and the arithmetic that relates them, which is the actual claim.

**Two pre-existing flakes surfaced under full-suite parallelism, neither mine.**
`tests/razorpayPayouts.test.ts` fails roughly one run in three — reproduced on a
stashed tree, and it is the shared-payout-batch hazard CLAUDE.md already
documents. `tests/realtimeAdapter.test.ts` occasionally logs an unhandled
ioredis "Connection is closed" during teardown; both files pass alone.

### Concepts now genuinely in use
Nothing new. Three applications of one old idea: a decision the system reached
correctly is worthless until an operator can see it, which is invariant 8 read
as a design rule rather than as a checklist item.

### Interview angle
"Your system correctly worked out that no crew existed. What did the operator
see?" — nothing, for four phases, and the reason is worth more than the fix: the
escalation card had been built, was good, and was reachable only from a job
nobody had been told to click. Every one of the eight rejection codes was
already computed, stored and discarded at the last step. And: "how did a
coordinate 1,700km away get a five-minute ETA?" — three layers each
accommodating an impossible input, and the only one that behaved perfectly, the
map, was the one that made it visible.

